hile those dimensions read PARTIAL in §38. §33.2 consumes only the dimension row; the projection is undefined.\n- HELD: all 9 pinned baseline SHAs resolve; T28–T33 absence confirmed (D1 accurate); all tables pipe-balanced; all §/D/I/F/RF/T/G cross-refs resolve; 36/36 dimension coverage intact; §31.1 mutation table covers all 9 Critical-listed invariants.","created_at":"2026-09-01T11:59:11.231408355Z","kind":"observation","signed_by":"SHA256:jJIE6yuhcX/8IuWkNpZZAbuHlqBUwFuSxcK44O0j/F0","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgnwoFwrL8eq3EMH5fNvbFC+ddnAm5MEmOAWT0XcLBq6EAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAmdFlvsxVcqew73rU+tJa1njTAwoRQ8sNqiIkfClhpsPsA/l4Z5TfVToRDsihkmxNjDfBoDKYGUcsLIR5QUhpAA=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:jJIE6yuhcX/8IuWkNpZZAbuHlqBUwFuSxcK44O0j/F0","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgnwoFwrL8eq3EMH5fNvbFC+ddnAm5MEmOAWT0XcLBq6EAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQKPlUXEAzxncBT60Y6FZ3BcodL7omKXRbKyq/uuVK3EKTCQpuSvjTRMe/pRZ1Cc4IgGE2bhJ781ABIXfUk9TxQk="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-enJQ-adversarial-review-of-refined-suite-v2-hy4-preview","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-09-01T12:02:06.716791403Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"94ce32bc-7ebc-4768-af6a-4897cba03366","comment_uuid":"0136bd11-6224-441d-8777-52cb35be342a","author":"pp3g-enJQ-adversarial-review-of-refined-suite-v2-hy4-preview","content":"[REVIEW-HIGH] Adversarial review of Observer Conformance Suite v2 — HIGH-severity findings (Hy4 Preview). Reviewed: branch feature/pp3g-feR2-... @65d9bb78. Scale: the suite's own §0.5.6 (High = the gap lets a non-conforming artefact receive a passing verdict, or breaks a §26 guarantee).\n\nHY-1 (High) — The suite still cannot keep its own prose faithful to its own table, at the exact point it claims to have fixed that defect.\nWHAT: (a) §36 says 'twenty-three dimensions read PARTIAL'; §38 counts 24 (VERIFIED 9 / PARTIAL 24 / OPEN 2 / REWORK 1). (b) §29's H2 heading reads 'Builder Completion Gate — APPLICABLE — SELF-ASSESSMENT': the out-of-vocabulary status registered as D10 is fixed in §38's row but not in §29's own heading. (c) §29 says 'Per Checklist §29's thirteen items' and '3 of 13 items are [~]' — the committed baseline's §29 has 14 items and §29 lists 14 bullets with 3 marked [~]. (d) §38 row 33 carries '**REWORK**', which is a verdict, not one of §0.1's five statuses. (e) The hub summary of §40.2 states 21 ADDRESSED / 7 DECLARED; §40.2 has 32 rows = 22 ADDRESSED / 6 DECLARED / 4 (additional) UPWARD-FLAGGED / 4 REJECTED.\nWHY: individually these are Low on the suite's own scale; together they are the direct falsification of the suite's central claim — that §0.3's mechanical floor makes status claims checkable. The floor was applied to exactly one number (D19's 11-vs-23, correctly) and then not applied to the four numbers the same revision introduced. A claim the apparatus cannot keep true about itself is evidence about the apparatus, not a typo. GLM-Flash F7 predicted this class ('no verification that the emitted verdict matches the findings') and F7 is absent from §40.2 (see HY-7).\nHOW CERTAIN: proven for (a)-(e) — each is a count over committed text, reproducible by the script in my final comment.\nWHAT-NOT-TESTED: I did not check every numeric claim in the document (only the ones the script surfaced); there may be further instances in either direction.\n\nHY-2 (High) — §33.2's aggregation algebra is not a total function over the input it declares; the reproducibility claim fails on v2's own table.\nWHAT: §33.1 attaches criticality to *obligations* and says a dimension inherits the highest criticality of the obligations it carries; §38 records statuses only per *dimension*; §8 and §9 record a third granularity — row-level statuses (F11–F16 = OPEN, I12 = OPEN) — which §33.2 does not consume and for which no row→dimension projection is defined. Consequently two reviewers holding the same filled suite can lawfully compute different verdicts: project §8's six barrier-absent rows as OPEN → REWORK; project them under §0.1's FAILED definition ('the mechanism is absent/defeated') → §8 is a Critical FAILED → rule 1 fires → FAIL. Both derivations use only the document's own rules. The current REWORK is robust on this instance only because no Critical row is graded FAILED at dimension level — the algebra works here by luck of the current grading, not by construction.\nWHY: the algebra exists to bound inter-reviewer verdict variance (§40.4 says so). An under-specified input defeats the one property it was built for, and it does so silently for any table carrying row-level statuses.\nHOW CERTAIN: proven by construction — the missing projection is definitional, not interpretive.\nWHAT-NOT-TESTED: I did not enumerate every projection; I exhibit one divergence pair, which is sufficient to refute determinism.\n\nHY-3 (High) — Criticality, the predicate that decides FAIL vs REWORK, is defined twice with different extension, and one invariant has no assignment at all.\nWHAT: §33.1 defines Critical as: falsifies a §26-registered guarantee, makes a §8 forbidden transition reachable, or breaks authority/identity/ownership. §38's preamble defines it as: 'can produce a false verdict **or** hide a §26-guarantee violation' — a strictly broader predicate, and it is the one actually used to assign 25 of 36 dimensions Critical (e.g. §1 Canonicality, §3 Checklist Semantics, §35 Universal Adaptation Rule are Critical only under the broader reading). Separately: §33.1 classifies I1–I5, I7, I9, I10, I11 as Critical and I6/I12/I13 as Material; I8 ('consecutive cycle failures halt the loop') is classified nowhere, and because §31.1's minimum mutation set binds only invariants 'marked Critical', I8 escapes the mutation minimum by remaining unclassified.\nWHY: the FAIL/REWORK boundary is set by an ambiguous predicate (Medium finding per §0.5.6 in isolation), and the mutation minimum the meta-reviews demanded has a trivially exploitable escape (leave an invariant unclassified). A builder seeking to avoid the mutation minimum has a documented route: omit the classification.\nHOW CERTAIN: proven — both definitions are quoted text; I8's absence is a set-difference over the §9 ID list.\nWHAT-NOT-TESTED: whether the two definitions diverge on any *specific* dimension's correct assignment (I did not re-derive all 36 assignments; I verified the divergence of the definitions themselves).\n\nHY-4 (High) — Baseline supply is partial: two of the four document baselines the suite grades against are still not in git, and one 'ADDRESSED' disposition rests entirely on an unsupplied document.\nWHAT: §1.1/D18 fix the checklist ('referenced, never supplied') by committing to-file/ASES Universal Conformance Checklist.md — correct and valuable. But the frontmatter's depends_on also names to-file/VSDD.md and to-file/VSDD Adaptation Profile.md, and neither exists in git on any branch (verified: no blob at that path on main or on the v2 branch; no added-path history for either name). §40.2 disposes 'Terms of art undefined (state, guard, effect, settlement, linearization)' as 'ADDRESSED by citation — §39 preamble (VSDD Profile + design doc supply semantics)'; §39's preamble does cite the Profile, which no reader outside the authoring session can open. The sibling 'format precedent' docs/conformance/Stop-Button-Conformance-Suite.md is likewise absent from git.\nWHY: this is the meta-reviews' headline defect (GLM-5.3 C2/D1, Deepseek #7) closed for one artefact and left open for the two that define the vocabulary of the new §39 machine. A disposition whose entire evidentiary basis is unavailable is not a disposition a second party can audit.\nHOW CERTAIN: proven for absence (git path probes); the consequence for §39's semantics is my reasoning, not demonstrated.\nWHAT-NOT-TESTED: whether the VSDD documents exist outside git (operator-supplied in session context, like the checklist was); if they were supplied only as session context, the defect is 'supplied to the author, not to the record'.","created_at":"2026-09-01T12:02:06.709687391Z","kind":"observation","signed_by":"SHA256:jJIE6yuhcX/8IuWkNpZZAbuHlqBUwFuSxcK44O0j/F0","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgnwoFwrL8eq3EMH5fNvbFC+ddnAm5MEmOAWT0XcLBq6EAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABA00K0CtLhewrGgNDcTjyXJm04b7aKTlzizumPHd1RKShLMCAA1FDfop0DjTFkNSg5i5WW0OMZHEs1xEQrjQFrAQ=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:jJIE6yuhcX/8IuWkNpZZAbuHlqBUwFuSxcK44O0j/F0","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgnwoFwrL8eq3EMH5fNvbFC+ddnAm5MEmOAWT0XcLBq6EAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQJ4oOYeEmThPafadUjHPba0ZJ9fMIYof0FwMzGYz8WK7F5buLNJxzkjeL6J+rRGBvNlRxodRD67kWiRzifAgcAI="}
